 "notes": [
  "Swahili subject-marker/TAM tables are standard reference grammar (version-independent).",
  "Kitaita candidates are induced from paradigmatic contrast across Swahili root groups (kitaita_verb_paradigm.py method), run separately per version then merged: a candidate segment supported by MULTIPLE independently-worded versions is more likely a real morpheme than one supported by repeated examples within a single version's register.",
  "Negation: only the two bare markers (general 'ha-', 1sg-irregular 'si-') are listed, per direct user request -- added as reference grammar (Ashton 1944; Mohammed 2001), not derived from or checked against this corpus. The full negative-TAM paradigm (compound forms like sija-/haku-/hawaja-) and any Kitaita negation marker are still not attempted -- known, flagged gap.",
  "Candidate Kitaita subject-marker prefix available for only 14/15 classes (listed above) -- used on Page 2 as an exploratory within-Kitaita agreement cross-check on noun class hypotheses; classes without a candidate here get no such check, not a silent pass.",
  "2026-10-07, NATIVE-SPEAKER CORRECTION: conditional is ki OR ka; perfect is da; past is re OR de; present is we OR zero-marked (like Swahili's own occasional zero-marked present); future is cha. This REPLACES the earlier induced guess (uki-/wada-/ore-ieghe) as the primary hypothesis for 'kitaita_tam_table_corrected' above -- the old induced table is kept in kitaita_verb_paradigms.json for the record, not deleted.",
  "Re-decomposed the full pooled Kitaita vocabulary with the corrected table: {'present': 16605, 'conditional': 3709, 'future': 1891, 'past': 46, 'perfect': 12} word TYPES per label (NOT occurrence-weighted -- 'present/zero' dominates because it is the always-available fallback whenever no explicit TAM fits, so treat its count as 'ambiguous/unmarked', not a real signal of how common the present tense actually is).",
  "INVESTIGATED the conditional ki/ka split rather than treating it as free variation (user's request): 'ka' outnumbers 'ki' roughly 7-8:1 in raw occurrences, and BOTH variants occur after every candidate subject-marker prefix (not cleanly split by class) -- i.e. this does NOT look like phonologically-conditioned allomorphy of ONE category. Spot-checking real examples instead: 'ka' examples are narrative chains ('Mlungu ukaghamba...' 'Mlungu ukawona...' = 'and then God said/saw...'); 'ki' examples are simultaneous/manner clauses ('...ukimzera' = 'saying to him', i.e. 'while saying'). This is the SAME two-way distinction Swahili itself independently makes with its OWN -ka- (narrative/consecutive 'and then') vs -ki- (conditional/temporal/converbal 'if/when/while') -- already noted as a plausible shared retention in this project's 2026-10-05 findings. Hypothesis to put to the native speaker: 'ki' and 'ka' may be two DIFFERENT grammatical categories (converbal/conditional vs narrative-consecutive), not two allomorphs of one 'conditional' slot -- see 'allomorphy_investigation.conditional_ki_ka' for the full subject-marker breakdown and example sentences to check this against.",
  "INVESTIGATED the past re/de split: unlike ki/ka, this DOES look subject-marker-conditioned -- class 8 ('ve-') uses 're' in 60/61 (98%) of its occurrences; class 3/11 ('ku-') and class 2 ('wi-') use 'de' in 55/61 (90%) and 15/15 (100%) respectively; class 15 ('ghu-') uses 're' in 6/6 (100%). Classes with few occurrences (class 1 'u-': 7 total; class 4/9 'i-': 9 total) are too thin to characterize confidently (project rule: no claims on n<8) and are reported as counts only, not a verdict. CAVEAT found while building this, not hidden: some 'de' matches are a likely segmentation collision with ROOTS that happen to start with the syllable 'de-' themselves (e.g. 'kudedanya' = 'to converse' -- the 'de' there is the start of the root 'dedany-', not the past-tense marker), the same kind of ambiguity already documented for Swahili's class-5 'li-' vs the past-tense '-li-' marker. See 'allomorphy_investigation.past_re_de' for the subject-marker breakdown, example sentences, and the (noisy, small-sample) governing-noun lists.",
  "Re-ran the Kitaita<->Swahili verb alignment at the ROOT level using the corrected TAM table (strips subject+TAM from BOTH sides before aligning, instead of matching whole inflected surface forms) -- 9039 Kitaita roots aligned, results folded into Page 1's verb table as 'root_level_kitaita_candidates'. This pools every inflection of a root into one count and is the more reliable of the two verb-translation methods on this page now (see Page 1 notes for the 'kuw'/'be' example this fixed)."
 ]
